1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:03,952
This sunflower is built
one seed at a time.

2
00:00:04,272 --> 00:00:07,941
Every new seed turns by the
same amount from the last one.

3
00:00:08,261 --> 00:00:09,691
That is the whole rule.

4
00:00:10,011 --> 00:00:12,891
Turn it half a degree
less, and gaps open up.

5
00:00:13,211 --> 00:00:17,712
Turn it a little more, and the
flower falls apart into five spokes.

6
00:00:18,932 --> 00:00:23,668
The plant uses a turn of 137.5 degrees.

7
00:00:23,988 --> 00:00:25,908
Not 137.

8
00:00:26,228 --> 00:00:28,980
Not a round 144.

9
00:00:29,300 --> 00:00:30,836
Why that one number?

10
00:00:32,056 --> 00:00:34,019
Stay to the end, and you will know.

11
00:00:34,339 --> 00:00:38,115
The answer is a number that
refuses to be a fraction.

12
00:00:39,335 --> 00:00:42,449
Here is the rule, drawn
the way our lab draws it.

13
00:00:42,769 --> 00:00:44,561
Seed one sits in the middle.

14
00:00:44,881 --> 00:00:48,401
Each new seed turns, and
sits a little further out.

15
00:00:49,621 --> 00:00:52,075
The lab gives every flower a score.

16
00:00:52,395 --> 00:00:56,875
It checks the gap between each seed and
its nearest neighbour, ring by ring.

17
00:00:57,195 --> 00:00:59,285
A perfect honeycomb scores 1.

18
00:00:59,605 --> 00:01:02,101
The flower's score is its worst ring.

19
00:01:03,321 --> 00:01:06,692
Most people guess that a neat
turn makes a neat flower.

20
00:01:07,012 --> 00:01:08,655
A third of a circle.

21
00:01:08,975 --> 00:01:09,999
Two fifths.

22
00:01:10,319 --> 00:01:12,025
Clean, simple numbers.

23
00:01:13,245 --> 00:01:15,208
So try a third of a turn.

24
00:01:15,528 --> 00:01:17,683
That is 120 degrees.

25
00:01:18,003 --> 00:01:20,413
What shape do 500 seeds make?

26
00:01:20,733 --> 00:01:21,949
Pause and guess.

27
00:01:26,469 --> 00:01:27,984
Three straight spokes.

28
00:01:28,304 --> 00:01:32,741
Every third seed points the same
way, so the seeds stack in lines.

29
00:01:33,061 --> 00:01:35,813
The score is 0.037.

30
00:01:37,033 --> 00:01:40,788
Two fifths of a turn is 144 degrees.

31
00:01:41,108 --> 00:01:44,927
Five spokes, and a score of 0.062.

32
00:01:45,247 --> 00:01:48,404
Three eighths is 135 degrees.

33
00:01:48,724 --> 00:01:51,753
Eight spokes, 0.099.

34
00:01:52,973 --> 00:01:54,979
Every neat fraction does this.

35
00:01:55,299 --> 00:02:00,547
The seeds come back to the same direction,
and the space between the spokes stays empty.

36
00:02:01,767 --> 00:02:03,516
So the neat guess fails.

37
00:02:03,836 --> 00:02:05,948
Fine, pick a messy turn instead.

38
00:02:06,268 --> 00:02:11,516
137 degrees is not a third, or
two fifths, or three eighths.

39
00:02:11,836 --> 00:02:13,159
Surely it works.

40
00:02:14,379 --> 00:02:17,003
It scores 0.563.

41
00:02:17,323 --> 00:02:18,581
Better, but look.

42
00:02:18,901 --> 00:02:23,339
The seeds curl into 21 arms,
with dark gaps between them.

43
00:02:24,559 --> 00:02:26,329
Now add half a degree.

44
00:02:26,649 --> 00:02:29,828
137.51 degrees.

45
00:02:30,148 --> 00:02:34,479
The score jumps to 0.884,
and the gaps close.

46
00:02:35,699 --> 00:02:37,597
Here is the strange part.

47
00:02:37,917 --> 00:02:43,123
137.51 scores 0.884.

48
00:02:43,443 --> 00:02:48,861
Add just 0.09 degrees, to 137.6.

49
00:02:49,181 --> 00:02:50,931
What happens to the score?

50
00:02:51,251 --> 00:02:52,467
Pause and guess.

51
00:02:56,987 --> 00:02:59,483
It drops to 0.522.

52
00:02:59,803 --> 00:03:01,957
Worse than 137.

53
00:03:02,277 --> 00:03:05,989
A tiny nudge, and 34
arms with gaps appear.

54
00:03:07,209 --> 00:03:09,001
So messy is not enough.

55
00:03:09,321 --> 00:03:12,948
One messy turn works, and its
next door neighbour fails.

56
00:03:13,268 --> 00:03:14,015
Why?

57
00:03:15,235 --> 00:03:18,093
Look at 137 degrees again.

58
00:03:18,413 --> 00:03:21,592
It sits very close to eight
twenty-firsts of a turn.

59
00:03:21,912 --> 00:03:25,368
That is 137.14 degrees.

60
00:03:25,688 --> 00:03:29,933
So every 21st seed comes back
almost to the same direction.

61
00:03:30,253 --> 00:03:32,216
Those are the 21 arms.

62
00:03:33,436 --> 00:03:38,961
137.6 sits close to thirteen
thirty-fourths of a turn.

63
00:03:39,281 --> 00:03:42,695
That is 137.65 degrees.

64
00:03:43,015 --> 00:03:45,681
So every 34th seed lines up.

65
00:03:46,001 --> 00:03:48,199
Those are the 34 arms.

66
00:03:49,419 --> 00:03:51,872
Every turn sits near some fraction.

67
00:03:52,192 --> 00:03:55,435
The nearer it sits, the
sooner the seeds line up.

68
00:03:55,755 --> 00:03:59,637
So the best turn is the one that
stays far from every fraction.

69
00:04:00,857 --> 00:04:02,863
Mathematicians know that number.

70
00:04:03,183 --> 00:04:06,788
It is the golden ratio, 1.618.

71
00:04:07,108 --> 00:04:13,465
Split a full circle by it, and
the smaller part is 137.5 degrees.

72
00:04:13,785 --> 00:04:15,471
That is the golden angle.

73
00:04:15,791 --> 00:04:18,223
No fraction gets close to it quickly.

74
00:04:19,443 --> 00:04:21,960
Watch the best fractions try to catch it.

75
00:04:22,280 --> 00:04:23,304
Two fifths.

76
00:04:23,624 --> 00:04:24,755
Three eighths.

77
00:04:25,075 --> 00:04:26,376
Five thirteenths.

78
00:04:26,696 --> 00:04:28,168
Eight twenty-firsts.

79
00:04:28,488 --> 00:04:30,131
Thirteen thirty-fourths.

80
00:04:30,451 --> 00:04:32,136
Twenty-one fifty-fifths.

81
00:04:32,456 --> 00:04:34,248
Thirty-four eighty-ninths.

82
00:04:34,568 --> 00:04:36,424
Above, below, above, below.

83
00:04:36,744 --> 00:04:38,024
Always a miss.

84
00:04:39,244 --> 00:04:41,185
Now read the bottom numbers.

85
00:04:41,505 --> 00:04:46,113
5, 8, 13, 21, 34, 55, 89.

86
00:04:46,433 --> 00:04:48,481
They are the Fibonacci numbers.

87
00:04:48,801 --> 00:04:50,700
The plant never counts them.

88
00:04:51,020 --> 00:04:52,727
They fall out of one turn.

89
00:04:53,947 --> 00:04:59,429
Near the rim of 500 seeds, neighbours
sit 34 seeds apart one way.

90
00:04:59,749 --> 00:05:02,331
And 55 seeds apart the other way.

91
00:05:02,651 --> 00:05:06,789
So you can count 34
spirals, and 55 spirals.

92
00:05:08,009 --> 00:05:10,463
Now double the seeds, to 1,000.

93
00:05:10,783 --> 00:05:12,980
How many spirals near the rim?

94
00:05:13,300 --> 00:05:14,516
Pause and guess.

95
00:05:19,036 --> 00:05:20,871
55 and 89.

96
00:05:21,191 --> 00:05:23,409
The next pair of Fibonacci numbers.

97
00:05:23,729 --> 00:05:26,716
And the score stays at 0.884.

98
00:05:27,936 --> 00:05:29,301
That is the secret.

99
00:05:29,621 --> 00:05:32,757
The golden angle never
lines up, at any size.

100
00:05:33,077 --> 00:05:36,277
So the head fills evenly,
however big it grows.

101
00:05:37,497 --> 00:05:40,079
You can try all of this in our free lab.

102
00:05:40,399 --> 00:05:43,727
Start at 137 degrees and see the gaps.

103
00:05:44,047 --> 00:05:49,913
Slide to 137.51, and the
arms melt into an even head.

104
00:05:50,233 --> 00:05:52,879
Press two fifths, and
the spokes come back.

105
00:05:54,099 --> 00:05:56,381
Real sunflowers are not perfect.

106
00:05:56,701 --> 00:06:04,125
In one citizen science study, the public grew
sunflowers, and 657 seed heads were checked.

107
00:06:04,445 --> 00:06:10,291
Of 768 spiral counts, 74%
were Fibonacci numbers.

108
00:06:10,611 --> 00:06:11,848
Most, not all.

109
00:06:13,068 --> 00:06:16,396
So why 137.5 degrees?

110
00:06:16,716 --> 00:06:20,257
Because it is the turn that is
hardest to write as a fraction.

111
00:06:20,577 --> 00:06:24,289
So the seeds never line
up, and no space is wasted.

112
00:06:25,509 --> 00:06:27,664
Here is one thing to try today.

113
00:06:27,984 --> 00:06:31,163
Find a sunflower, a pine
cone, or a pineapple.

114
00:06:31,483 --> 00:06:34,640
Count the spirals going
one way, then the other.

115
00:06:34,960 --> 00:06:40,165
You will often get two neighbours
from 5, 8, 13, 21, 34.

116
00:06:41,385 --> 00:06:45,417
The lab is free, at cosmos
dot rimilab dot com.

117
00:06:45,737 --> 00:06:48,724
Next week, back to business,
with a money puzzle.

